    card title "Why did Rome fall?"

A: Why did the Western Roman Empire collapse — and why only the western half?
    draw map

B: Easy. Lead pipes. They poisoned themselves.
    draw sus_box sus_line_2

A: Lovely theory. But the East had the same pipes, and (@draw x_lead@) it lasted another thousand years.

A: In 1984 the German historian Alexa (@draw sus_line_1@)nder Demandt listed every cause people had blamed: two hundred and ten of them.

B: Then I'll take two more off the menu: orgies, and Christianity. Gibbon blamed that one.
    draw sus_line_3 sus_line_4

    draw x_orgies x_christ

A: Gibbon did, in part. But two hundred and ten causes means nobody has found the one cause. So let's follow the money.
    blocking false

    highlight sus_line_3 sus_line_4

    pause 2

    highlight sus_line_1 effect circle

    pause 2

    point at.ref sus_box gesture underline

    erase sus x_lead x_orgies x_christ

A: Around the year 200 the empire ringed t (@draw fr_west fr_hadrian fr_east fr_persia persia_lbl sea_lbl@)he Mediterranean, from northern Britain to the Euphrates: a sea the Romans just called [it-IT:Mare Nostrum], our sea.

    erase sea_lbl

    hide map fr_west fr_hadrian fr_east fr_persia persia_lbl

A: Then, from 235 to 284, it nearly fell apart: civil wars, plague, and silver coins w (@draw coin ends@)ith hardly any silver left.

B: So how many would-be emperors does fifty years of chaos get you?
    draw claim

    ask "Click the line at your guess." on claim store g default "20" after keep
        right: Close: you said {g}. More than fifty men claimed the throne, and about half were recognised by the Senate.
        wrong: You said {g}. In fact more than fifty men claimed the throne, and about half were recognised by the Senate.

A: And most recognised emperors died violently, usually  (@draw crowns crown_t@)at the hands of their own soldiers. In 238 alone there were six.

B: Six in one year. That's not a throne, that's a revolving door.
    highlight crowns effect pulse

    erase claim coin crowns crown_t

    show map fr_west fr_hadrian fr_east fr_persia persia_lbl

A: Diocletian, emperor from 284, shared out the rule; i (@draw split c_cple c_cple_l west_lbl east_lbl@)n 330 Constantine built a new capital in the East, Constantinople, today's Istanbul; and after 395 the split was for good.

B: Same empire, two halves. Which half would you rather inherit?
    blocking false

    point at.ref west_lbl gesture circle

    pause 1

    point at.ref east_lbl gesture circle

A: The East, every time. Egypt (@draw lab_egypt lab_syria lab_asia@), Syria and Asia Minor had the richest cities and farms, and paid the most tax.

    draw c_walls

A: The West had the exposed frontier, the Rhine and the upper Danube; the East had the lower Danube and Persia to watch, and its rich heartland sat behind the sea and Constantinople's walls.
    blocking false

    highlight fr_west fr_hadrian

    pause 3

    highlight fr_east

    pause 1.4

    highlight fr_persia persia_lbl

    pause 1.6

    highlight c_walls effect circle

B: Fine. But in the end the barbarians just walked in, right?
    point at.ref fr_east gesture underline

    draw huns_l huns goths goths_l

A: Not quite walked. In 376 the Huns, horsemen from the steppe, drove Goths to the Danube, and the Goths asked to be let in.
    blocking false

    flow huns

    pause 1.5

    flow goths

    pause 1

    point at.ref fr_east gesture underline

    draw c_adri c_adri_l parallel true

A: Two years later, at Adrianople, those Goths destroyed an eastern Roman army and killed its emperor, Valens. Later, under Alaric, they moved west.
    blocking false

    highlight c_adri_l effect pulse

    pause 2

    point at.ref split gesture underline

    draw c_rome c_rome_l parallel true

A: In 410 the Visigoths sacked Rome itself, the first time in almost eight hundred years.
    blocking false

    point at.ref c_rome gesture circle

    pause 1.2

    highlight c_rome_l

B: So that's the fall, then. Roll credits.
    erase huns huns_l goths goths_l c_adri c_adri_l

    draw c_ravenna c_ravenna_l parallel true

A: Not yet. Rome wasn't even the capital any more: the western court sat in Ravenna, safe behind its marshes.
    blocking false

    pause 1.4

    point at.ref c_rome gesture tap

    pause 1

    point at.ref c_ravenna gesture circle

A: The blow that really hurt was a loss of land. (@draw ch_britain ch_gaul ch_africa@) Which loss do you think hurt the West most?

    ask "Tap the one you think hurt most." choose ["ch_britain","ch_gaul","ch_africa"] answer ch_africa stage own reveal "Africa, 439"
        right: Africa. In 439 the Vandals took Carthage, and with it the West's richest tax-paying provinces and much of Rome's grain.
        wrong: Think about which provinces paid the most tax, and fed Rome.

    erase ch_britain ch_gaul ch_africa

B: Vandals. The name says it all.
    draw africa_shade c_carthage c_carthage_l

A: A bit unfair: the word vandalism was only coined in 1794. But yes,  (@draw sack455@)in 455 they sailed over and looted Rome for two weeks.

    erase sus_box sus_line_2 sus_line_1 sus_line_3 sus_line_4 map fr_west fr_hadrian fr_east fr_persia persia_lbl split c_cple c_cple_l west_lbl east_lbl lab_egypt lab_syria lab_asia c_walls c_rome c_rome_l c_ravenna c_ravenna_l africa_shade c_carthage c_carthage_l sack455

A: So follow the money. An empire is a machine for turning taxes into soldiers, and without Africa the West couldn't pay enough of them.
    draw west_head n_money a1 n_army

A: So it hired whole barbarian peoples as allies — federates — who fought under their own kings.
    draw a2 n_allies

B: Hiring the people you're fighting. What could go wrong?
    highlight n_allies

A: Plenty. Allies were paid in land, and land handed over paid no more taxes.
    draw a3 n_land a4

B: So: less money, a weaker army, more deals, less land. A spiral.
    flow a1 a2 a3 a4 delivery grave

A: The East had better money. It could pay its own armies, and even pay attackers like Attila to look elsewhere.
    draw east_head e_money e_army e_control

    erase west_head n_money a1 n_army a2 n_allies a3 n_land a4 east_head e_money e_army e_control

A: By 476 the West was a shell. A general called Odoacer deposed the last western emperor, a boy.
    draw tl_axis tl_ticks bar_w bar_w_l m476 m476_l

B: Romulus Augustulus, right? Rome's founder and its first emperor in one name, and they called him little Augustus.
    highlight m476_l

A: Odoacer didn't even bother making himself emperor. He sent the imperial regalia east and ruled Italy as king, nominally under Constantinople.
    blocking false

    point at.ref bar_w at.anchor right gesture circle

    pause 2

    highlight m476

    pause 2

    point at.ref tkl_400 gesture tap

A: The East went on for almost another thousand years, until the Ottomans took Constantinople in 1453.
    draw bar_e bar_e_l m1453 m1453_l

B: So the Roman Empire didn't fall. Half of it just moved to Istanbul.
    highlight bar_e

A: Historians still argue about t (@draw wp_pot wp_coin wp_cow@)he word fall. Some see a slow transformation; Bryan Ward-Perkins points to cruder pots, fewer coins, even smaller cattle.

    move wp scale 0.55 duration 1.5

    erase tl_axis tl_ticks bar_w bar_w_l m476 m476_l bar_e bar_e_l m1453 m1453_l wp_pot wp_coin wp_cow

A: But our answer is no single cause. (@draw west_head n_money a1 n_army a2 n_allies a3 n_land a4 east_head e_money e_army e_control delivery grave@) The West lost its money, then its army, then its grip; the East kept all three.

    quiz "Around 450, which would have done most to save the West?" reveal "468: Cape Bon"
        right: Winning back Africa: its taxes paid for armies. East and West tried in 468 with over a thousand ships, and lost the fleet off Cape Bon.
        wrong: Think about what paid the soldiers.
        * Rip out the lead pipes
        + Win back Africa
        * Stronger walls round Rome

